1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/MetaGrenade/laravel-vue/commits/a135f38d1ce355876184c21356775c90d5c76fb1"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/6120506?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MetaGrenade/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MetaGrenade/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MetaGrenade/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MetaGrenade/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/MetaGrenade","id":6120506,"login":"MetaGrenade","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjYxMjA1MDY=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MetaGrenade/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MetaGrenade/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MetaGrenade/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MetaGrenade/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MetaGrenade/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MetaGrenade","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/MetaGrenade/laravel-vue/commits/34f70665ca2884df22f0aaecea54781e1a578b8f/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-07T01:01:51Z","email":"fragzone.caleb@gmail.com","name":"Caleb Harrod"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-07T01:01:51Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"M1a - Payments foundation, Stripe checkout and orders (#252)\n\n* M1a: payments foundation, Stripe checkout and orders\n\nFirst slice of milestone M1. A guest or signed-in customer can check out a\ncart with Stripe; the order becomes paid when the webhook arrives.\n\nPayments\n- Provider-neutral layer in App\\Payments: a PaymentProvider contract,\n  PaymentManager, StripeProvider (hosted Stripe Checkout) and a store-level\n  provider setting in the ACP. Tebex plugs in behind the same contract in M2.\n- Idempotent webhooks: every delivery is stored under (provider, external_id),\n  redeliveries are acknowledged without reprocessing, failures are recorded and\n  retried by the provider. billing_webhook_calls is generalised in place.\n- A paid session must match the order''s amount and currency or the order is\n  flagged for review instead of fulfilled. The provider refuses to create a\n  session whose lines do not add up to the order total.\n\nOrders and stock\n- OrderPlacer re-prices everything from the catalogue and holds stock with one\n  conditional UPDATE per line. Every change is written to inventory_movements.\n- OrderLifecycle is the only place an order changes state; each method locks the\n  row and is idempotent. Unpaid orders expire (ExpirePendingOrders, every five\n  minutes) after asking the provider whether they were paid. A payment for an\n  already cancelled order is honoured and flagged.\n- Orders and payments carry an owner morph and are authorised through a policy,\n  so teams can own orders in 1.1 without a rewrite.\n- Money value object for exact arithmetic in minor units, including\n  zero-decimal currencies.\n\nStorefront\n- Cart update and remove, checkout page, confirmation page (signed link for\n  guests, polling while the webhook is pending), receipt email, order history.\n- The header cart no longer shows an invented 7% tax and flat shipping charge.\n\nUpgrade\n- New migrations backfill existing orders and webhook calls; upgrade-path tests\n  cover both. Run php artisan migrate. Stripe needs the four checkout.session.*\n  events enabled; production needs the scheduler and a queue worker.\n\nAlso types the Cart, CartItem, OrderItem, Product, ProductVariant, Price and\nInventoryItem relations, which shrinks the PHPStan baseline from 604 to 592.\n\nSee docs/commerce.md.\n\n* M1a review fixes: one payable checkout per cart, cart after payment, status limiter\n\nReplacing a checkout could leave two payable sessions\n- Starting a new checkout cancelled the previous order immediately but only\n  expired its Stripe session later, from a queued listener. With the database\n  queue, or a delayed or failed worker, both sessions were payable, and paying\n  both produced two charged orders because the late-payment path reinstates a\n  cancelled order.\n- CheckoutStarter now closes the earlier checkout at the provider and waits for\n  the answer before creating a replacement. PaymentProvider::cancelCheckout()\n  (best effort) is replaced by closeCheckout(), which returns Closed, Paid or\n  Unresolved, or throws when the provider cannot confirm. Closed: replace. Paid:\n  show that order, start nothing. Unresolved or unreachable: replace nothing and\n  ask the shopper to retry. A payment held for review also blocks a new checkout.\n- A short per-cart lock stops two requests closing and creating checkouts at\n  once. OrderPlacer no longer cancels earlier orders itself. The queued listener\n  stays as a safety net for other cancellations and now retries on failure.\n\nCart edits made while payment was pending were lost\n- Paying marked the whole cart converted although the order only held the\n  snapshot from when checkout started, so products added meanwhile vanished.\n  Paying now removes only the ordered lines (a line is reduced when the cart held\n  more) and marks the cart converted only once nothing is left.\n\nConfirmation polling exceeded the billing rate limit\n- The completion page polled ten times in 40 seconds against the shared billing\n  limiter (10 a minute). It now has its own checkout-status limiter (30 a\n  minute, per order), and the page checks about seven times with backoff, each\n  check waiting for the previous one to finish.\n\nCI: MySQL migration tests\n- CheckoutMigrationsTest used DB::transaction() as a savepoint around statements\n  expected to fail. On MySQL the DDL in down() commits the test transaction\n  implicitly, so the savepoint did not exist. Only PostgreSQL, which needs the\n  savepoint, now gets one. Reproduced on MariaDB 11.4 with the old test code\n  (same SAVEPOINT trans2 error) and verified fixed; the full suite also passes\n  there (512 tests).\n\nTests: 23 new (replacement guarantees including a worker that never runs,\ncart consumption, limiter). 512 pass on SQLite and MariaDB.","tree":{"sha":"6132261de75fc33c2c6d0d8ae057e25b9eccb72e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/MetaGrenade/laravel-vue/git/trees/6132261de75fc33c2c6d0d8ae057e25b9eccb72e"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/MetaGrenade/laravel-vue/git/commits/34f70665ca2884df22f0aaecea54781e1a578b8f","verification":{"payload":"tree 6132261de75fc33c2c6d0d8ae057e25b9eccb72e\nparent a4759b3b3149d31169e00c77a7b019ab0e4d331a\nauthor Caleb Harrod <fragzone.caleb@gmail.com> 1791334911 -0600\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791334911 -0600\n\nM1a - Payments foundation, Stripe checkout and orders (#252)\n\n* M1a: payments foundation, Stripe checkout and orders\n\nFirst slice of milestone M1. A guest or signed-in customer can check out a\ncart with Stripe; the order becomes paid when the webhook arrives.\n\nPayments\n- Provider-neutral layer in App\\Payments: a PaymentProvider contract,\n  PaymentManager, StripeProvider (hosted Stripe Checkout) and a store-level\n  provider setting in the ACP. Tebex plugs in behind the same contract in M2.\n- Idempotent webhooks: every delivery is stored under (provider, external_id),\n  redeliveries are acknowledged without reprocessing, failures are recorded and\n  retried by the provider. billing_webhook_calls is generalised in place.\n- A paid session must match the order''s amount and currency or the order is\n  flagged for review instead of fulfilled. The provider refuses to create a\n  session whose lines do not add up to the order total.\n\nOrders and stock\n- OrderPlacer re-prices everything from the catalogue and holds stock with one\n  conditional UPDATE per line. Every change is written to inventory_movements.\n- OrderLifecycle is the only place an order changes state; each method locks the\n  row and is idempotent. Unpaid orders expire (ExpirePendingOrders, every five\n  minutes) after asking the provider whether they were paid. A payment for an\n  already cancelled order is honoured and flagged.\n- Orders and payments carry an owner morph and are authorised through a policy,\n  so teams can own orders in 1.1 without a rewrite.\n- Money value object for exact arithmetic in minor units, including\n  zero-decimal currencies.\n\nStorefront\n- Cart update and remove, checkout page, confirmation page (signed link for\n  guests, polling while the webhook is pending), receipt email, order history.\n- The header cart no longer shows an invented 7% tax and flat shipping charge.\n\nUpgrade\n- New migrations backfill existing orders and webhook calls; upgrade-path tests\n  cover both. Run php artisan migrate. Stripe needs the four checkout.session.*\n  events enabled; production needs the scheduler and a queue worker.\n\nAlso types the Cart, CartItem, OrderItem, Product, ProductVariant, Price and\nInventoryItem relations, which shrinks the PHPStan baseline from 604 to 592.\n\nSee docs/commerce.md.\n\n* M1a review fixes: one payable checkout per cart, cart after payment, status limiter\n\nReplacing a checkout could leave two payable sessions\n- Starting a new checkout cancelled the previous order immediately but only\n  expired its Stripe session later, from a queued listener. With the database\n  queue, or a delayed or failed worker, both sessions were payable, and paying\n  both produced two charged orders because the late-payment path reinstates a\n  cancelled order.\n- CheckoutStarter now closes the earlier checkout at the provider and waits for\n  the answer before creating a replacement. PaymentProvider::cancelCheckout()\n  (best effort) is replaced by closeCheckout(), which returns Closed, Paid or\n  Unresolved, or throws when the provider cannot confirm. Closed: replace. Paid:\n  show that order, start nothing. Unresolved or unreachable: replace nothing and\n  ask the shopper to retry. A payment held for review also blocks a new checkout.\n- A short per-cart lock stops two requests closing and creating checkouts at\n  once. OrderPlacer no longer cancels earlier orders itself. The queued listener\n  stays as a safety net for other cancellations and now retries on failure.\n\nCart edits made while payment was pending were lost\n- Paying marked the whole cart converted although the order only held the\n  snapshot from when checkout started, so products added meanwhile vanished.\n  Paying now removes only the ordered lines (a line is reduced when the cart held\n  more) and marks the cart converted only once nothing is left.\n\nConfirmation polling exceeded the billing rate limit\n- The completion page polled ten times in 40 seconds against the shared billing\n  limiter (10 a minute). It now has its own checkout-status limiter (30 a\n  minute, per order), and the page checks about seven times with backoff, each\n  check waiting for the previous one to finish.\n\nCI: MySQL migration tests\n- CheckoutMigrationsTest used DB::transaction() as a savepoint around statements\n  expected to fail. On MySQL the DDL in down() commits the test transaction\n  implicitly, so the savepoint did not exist. Only PostgreSQL, which needs the\n  savepoint, now gets one. Reproduced on MariaDB 11.4 with the old test code\n  (same SAVEPOINT trans2 error) and verified fixed; the full suite also passes\n  there (512 tests).\n\nTests: 23 new (replacement guarantees including a worker that never runs,\ncart consumption, limiter). 512 pass on SQLite and MariaDB.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxZn/CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAlNQQAELm5dZVQZiyPY5LdM+LTv01\nhsnlOdnmbuKFC2C1vxUaa4dIAGimWO2a7vGtiArfnV/wtTGj2PdeXUcymvT03VoI\nfD9aoS0ggpz2QVIgNekvpsKscQ9BQzY/UMbT1W/X9jL+E3lTi53kOvvzDMTOHPw1\nz1ITzh/65YF49wns+TYcSFWAsFdIB5Sp2ImEAwLq5M6qdOAL1zj1OyT8o+w0ro7G\nVCyAQ+Rv1mpm0ZURSHZwi/AqxNFy0NnneF8RGtYbvIVgWlWMSmzPC